From 4c9c26610521f63c7abbae40c9a04ecd27c12c27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 03:36:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: a switch of subtitle stream is shown from what
 was read, without reading the file again

The demuxer reads every stream of a file, and the player drops the packets of
the subtitle streams it does not show. On a switch to another stream, those of
its packets on screen now and the ones read since (the demuxer is ahead of
playback) were gone: the player took the demuxer back to the playback time
(RereadSubtitleData()) or, where it could not, seeked. From a streaming service
that is the file read again from a server that may allow one connection, and
for a PGS stream the display set on screen now was lost when it began before
the keyframe the demuxer went back to.

CSubtitleTrackMemory keeps the packets of every subtitle stream of the file as
they are read (PGS and text: SubRip, plain text, MOV text, ASS/SSA), from the
last discontinuity of the reading on (a seek, a new file), bounded to 60 s
before the playback time, 4 MiB per stream and 24 MiB in all. On a switch it
gives the stream what its decoder needs, through the player's own path for a
packet (ProcessSubData()):
- PGS: from its last display set at or before now whose composition state is
  not "normal" (an epoch start or an acquisition point: everything it shows
  is in it), or from now on when it has none and the memory began at least
  10 s before;
- text: its lines that end after now, when the memory began at least 10 s
  before;
and every packet after those up to the last one read, in order; the demuxer
goes on with the next. When the memory does not hold it, the switch is as
before. One INFO line per switch, and at the end of the file the most it
kept. Only for a file on the timeline of its demuxer, as RereadSubtitleData().

Measured: a film with six 1080p PGS tracks (about 12 KB a display set, one of
60 KB) read 8 s ahead of playback kept at most 1756 KiB, 2366 KiB read 30 s
ahead; 24 tracks 7010 and 9464 KiB (host test, display sets made as a disc's).
In a VM (an image of this tree before, a Matroska file with six PGS and two
SubRip streams from a server that behaves like a streaming CDN), eight switches
4 s apart: before, the demuxer taken back eight times and, for the two text
streams, a request to the add-on's resolver and four transfers of the file
(the search of CSubtitleSeekSearch); now, each of the eight shown from what
was read, nothing asked of the server, at most 1050 KiB kept.
Credit: the idea of a switch without a jump is mephis's
(flhs0606/LibreELEC-Hades, dcfd7435b4); written anew on Hades' seek memory of
text subtitles, for PGS too and bounded.
